I have a Java utility class that sorts and filters collections using raw types and unchecked casts, which leads to compiler warnings and potential ClassCastExceptions. Could you refactor the following code to use proper generics? Apply PECS for wildcard arguments, replace raw types with parameterized types, add bounded type parameters where appropriate, and eliminate unsafe casts. Also, ensure the class compiles cleanly with no unchecked warnings. Here's the class:

public class CollectionUtils {
    public static List filter(List list, Predicate predicate) {
        List result = new ArrayList();
        for (Object item : list) {
            if (predicate.test(item)) {
                result.add(item);
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static <T> void addAll(List list, Collection col) {
        for (Object item : col) {
            list.add(item);
        }
    }
}

Please rewrite this class with strong type safety, using the diamond operator, and ensure that the addAll method works with any Collection and List (using appropriate wildcards). The final code should compile under Java 17+.

